---
import { siteProfile } from "../data/site";
import FactsList from "../components/FactsList.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const quickFacts = [
  {
    label: "Affiliation",
    value: siteProfile.affiliation,
  },
  {
    label: "Research area",
    value: siteProfile.researchArea,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Research profile, current questions, and the kinds of quantum-circuit collaborations I am open to."
>
  <section class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      I work on the algebra of quantum circuits: when two circuits should count
      as the same, which equations are enough to prove it, and how those
      equations turn into normal forms, rewrite systems, or small tools.
    </p>

    <FactsList class="quick-facts" aria-label="Quick facts" facts={quickFacts} />

    <aside class="disambiguation" aria-labelledby="disambiguation-title">
      <h2 id="disambiguation-title">Disambiguation</h2>

      <p>
        Because &quot;Colin Blake&quot; turns out not to be a unique identifier, a brief
        clarification may help. I am not
        <a
          href="https://www.eliteprospects.com/player/240742/colin-blake"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >the hockey player Colin Blake</a>, nor
        <a
          href="https://www.imdb.com/name/nm2072003/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >the production designer Colin Blake.</a>
      </p>

      <p>
        Genealogically, I cannot currently prove that I am related to
        <a
          href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blake_(surname)"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          William Blake
        </a>
        , but the surname is at least suggestive enough to keep the joke alive.
        For further information on the wider Blake line, I can only recommend
        the impeccably reliable
        <a
          href="https://seanceofblakemanor.wiki.gg/wiki/Blake_Family"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Blake Family records from <em>The Seance at Blake Manor</em>
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </aside>

    <p>
      I am a PhD student in quantum computing at <strong>Inria</strong>, in
      the <strong>MOCQUA</strong> team. My research is about <strong>when two
      quantum circuits should count as the same</strong>, especially in qudit
      settings where the right algebra is still much less settled than in the
      qubit world.
    </p>

    <p>
      Because I am still a PhD student, I am not currently supervising students
      on my own. I am nevertheless happy to discuss related problems with other
      researchers, or with students who already have formal supervision elsewhere.
    </p>

    <p>
      In practice, that means asking which generators and relations actually
      describe a circuit fragment, how to make those descriptions uniform in
      the dimension, and how to turn them into normal forms, rewrite systems,
      or small pieces of software.
    </p>

    <h2>Current themes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>uniform presentations for qudit circuit families in arbitrary finite dimension</li>
      <li>phase-affine and controlled constructions that expose useful algebraic structure</li>
      <li>rewrite systems and diagrammatic languages that can support optimisation in a principled way</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="awards-and-funding">Awards and funding</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>2026</strong> —
        <a
          href="https://institutfrancais.nl/le-programme-de-bourses-france-excellence-quantum/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >France Excellence Quantum scholarship</a>
        for a research visit to the University of Amsterdam.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>2026</strong> —
        <a href="/publications#blake:LIPIcs.MFCS.2026.6">Best Student Paper award, MFCS 2026</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>2025</strong> — first prize of the jury at Scientific Game Jam
        Nancy, with the team behind
        <a href="/games/#les-chevaliers-du-quantique"><em>Les Chevaliers du Quantique</em></a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>2022–2023</strong> —
        <a
          href="https://digicosme.cnrs.fr/labex-digicosme-bourses-master/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >LabEx DigiCosme excellence scholarship</a>, awarded for my M1 MPRI.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Academic path</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>2024–2027 — PhD in quantum computing</strong> (ongoing) at Inria
      (MOCQUA), supervised by Simon Perdrix and Miriam Backens.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before starting the PhD, I moved through a sequence of internships that
      gradually narrowed from broad formal-methods questions to circuit syntax
      and completeness results.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>M2 research internship (2024)</strong> at Inria (MOCQUA), on
        completeness questions for qudit circuit theories.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>M1 research internship (2023)</strong> at Inria (QuaCS), on
        fragments of the ZX-calculus enriched with partial transpose.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>L3 research internship (2022)</strong> at LMF, on typed quantum
        circuit compilation and pattern-matching techniques for the ZX-calculus.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="education">Education</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>2023–2024 — M2 MPRI</strong>, ENS Paris-Saclay.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>2022–2023 — M1 MPRI</strong>, Université Paris-Saclay, in
        theoretical computer science.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>2021–2024 — Magistère d'Informatique</strong>, Université
        Paris-Saclay: a three-year university diploma spanning L3–M2.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>2019–2022 — Licence double-diplôme in Mathematics and Computer Science</strong>,
        Université Paris-Saclay.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="teaching">Teaching</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>2026–2027 — Mines Nancy</strong>: tutorials and practical classes (TD/TP)
        since September 2026 in <em>Algorithmie et complexité</em> and
        <em>Python pour l'ingénieur</em>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>2025–2026 — Université de Lorraine</strong> (September 2025–August
        2026): practical classes in Java software projects, introductory
        artificial intelligence (state-space search, A*, minimax, and alpha-beta
        pruning), and introductory algorithms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>2024–2025 — Université de Lorraine</strong> (February–August 2025):
        practical classes in Java software projects and introductory artificial
        intelligence.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <style>
    .quick-facts {
      margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    }

    .disambiguation {
      margin: 1.5rem 0;
      padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
      border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
      border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
      border-radius: 0.7rem;
      background:
        linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(15, 102, 124, 0.06), transparent 72%),
        var(--color-surface);
    }

    .disambiguation h2 {
      margin-top: 0;
      font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.4vw, 1.45rem);
    }

    .disambiguation p:last-child {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
